        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "01d832f7-e937-5c85-9781-5df82157429a",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Events: Church Patronage (Scotland) Act 1711, Glorious Revolution\n- Organizations: Church of Scotland\n- Roles: Patrons, noble patrons\n- People: James II of England, James VII of Scotland, William III, Prince of Orange\n- Places: Scotland, England\n\nFacts:\n- The Glorious Revolution, also called the Revolution of 1688, overthrew King James II of England, who was also James VII of Scotland.\n- The Glorious Revolution was carried out by a union of English Parliamentarians and the Dutch stadtholder William III, Prince of Orange.\n- During the Glorious Revolution, patrons in Scotland lost the custom of controlling Church of Scotland parish churches.\n- The Church Patronage (Scotland) Act 1711 was intended to let noble and other patrons in Scotland regain control over Church of Scotland parish churches.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "43c0ddcf-439b-5963-ac32-3763e24ec84c",
        "text": "Page: Church Patronage (Scotland) Act 1711. Sentence: Its purpose was to allow the noble and other Patrons in Scotland to gain control over the Church of Scotland parish churches again, having lost that custom in the so-called Glorious Revolution.\nPage: Glorious Revolution. Sentence: The Glorious Revolution, also called the Revolution of 1688, was the overthrow of King James II of England (James VII of Scotland) by a union of English Parliamentarians with the Dutch stadtholder William III, Prince of Orange.",
